{
  "concept": "In any oblique triangle the altitude h dropped from vertex β to the base b is the bridge to right-triangle trigonometry — h = c·sin(α) when α is acute, and h = c·sin(α′) on the extended base when α is obtuse, so the same area formula holds in both cases.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left panel: acute-case triangle with horizontal base from vertex α (lower left) to vertex γ (lower right), apex β above the interior of the base",
    "Left panel side labels: b (horizontal base, italic, below the base), c (short left side α→β), a (long right side β→γ)",
    "Left panel dashed vertical segment from β straight down to a foot point on the interior of b, labeled h",
    "Left panel small square right-angle marker at the foot of the altitude",
    "Left panel arc at vertex α marking the interior angle",
    "Right panel: obtuse-case triangle with the same base b from α to γ, apex β shifted left so it lies to the LEFT of α, making the angle at α obtuse",
    "Right panel side labels: b, c, a in the same relative positions",
    "Right panel dashed vertical segment from β down to a foot point lying outside the triangle, labeled h",
    "Right panel dashed horizontal extension of the base running leftward from α to the foot of the altitude",
    "Right panel small square right-angle marker where the altitude meets the dashed extension",
    "Right panel two arcs at the lower-left corner: interior angle α (between c and b, opening right) and supplementary angle α′ (between c and the dashed extension, opening left)",
    "Vertex letters β (top), α (lower left), γ (lower right) in italic Greek, both panels",
    "All strokes thin black on white; solid lines for triangle sides, dashed for altitude and base extension",
    "Optional annotation callouts: 'h = c sin α' (acute panel) and 'h = c sin α′, α′ = 180° − α' (obtuse panel)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "apexPosition",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Sliding the apex β horizontally shows the foot of the altitude migrating continuously along the base and then off its left end onto the dashed extension — the height construction never disappears, it just lands outside the triangle.",
      "label": "Horizontal position of vertex β (fraction of base b)",
      "range": [-0.45, 0.95, 0.01],
      "default": 0.32
    },
    {
      "id": "caseView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Naming the two cases makes explicit which angle is adjacent to the perpendicular: in the acute view α itself, in the obtuse view its supplement α′ on the dashed base extension.",
      "label": "Case at vertex α",
      "options": ["Acute α", "Obtuse α", "Compare side by side"],
      "default": "Acute α"
    },
    {
      "id": "buildAltitude",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Clicking the base b constructs the perpendicular, revealing the hidden right triangle with hypotenuse c and leg h that converts the oblique triangle into something trigonometry can measure."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "An oblique triangle",
      "narration": "A triangle with sides a, b, c and no right angle of its own. Vertex β sits above the base b, between α and γ. Nothing here lets us read off a height directly.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Acute α", "apexPosition": 0.32 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Drop the altitude",
      "narration": "Click the base: a dashed perpendicular h falls from β to a point inside b, with a right-angle marker at its foot. The left piece is now a right triangle with hypotenuse c and leg h, so h = c·sin α.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Acute α", "apexPosition": 0.32 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slide β toward α",
      "narration": "As β moves left, the foot of the altitude slides toward α and the angle at α opens wider. Watch the foot approach the left end of the base.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Acute α", "apexPosition": 0.05 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The foot leaves the base",
      "narration": "Push β past α and the angle at α becomes obtuse. The perpendicular now lands outside the triangle, so the base must be carried leftward by a dashed extension to catch it.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Obtuse α", "apexPosition": -0.3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Which angle gives the height?",
      "narration": "Two arcs appear at the lower left: the interior angle α and, beside it on the extension, α′ = 180° − α. The right triangle is built on α′, so h = c·sin α′ — and since sin α′ = sin α, the same formula survives.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Obtuse α", "apexPosition": -0.3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Both cases together",
      "narration": "Side by side, the two constructions are identical in spirit: one altitude, one right triangle, one height h = c·sin α feeding Area = ½·b·h.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Compare side by side", "apexPosition": -0.25 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The altitude construction never fails — when the angle at α turns obtuse the perpendicular simply lands on an extension of the base, and because sin α′ = sin(180° − α) = sin α, the height h = c·sin α (and hence Area = ½ b c sin α) is the same formula in both cases.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two thin-black-line triangles on white, drawn side by side with generous whitespace, matching the OpenStax plain-line style. Each shares a horizontal base labeled b running from α at lower left to γ at lower right; γ is far right so side a is long and shallow, side c is short and steep. Left panel: apex β sits roughly one-third along the base and well above it, dashed vertical altitude labeled h (label placed to the right of the dashed line) meets b at a small open square right-angle marker; a single arc at α marks the interior angle. Right panel: apex β sits to the LEFT of α and slightly higher, so the angle at α is clearly obtuse; the dashed altitude drops from β to a foot on a dashed horizontal extension of the base that runs leftward past α; the same small square marks the right angle at that foot. At the lower-left corner of the right panel draw two adjacent arcs — α′ between c and the leftward extension (label α′ just left of and above the corner) and α between c and b (label α just right of it). All Greek vertex letters and side letters italic. When the altitude is un-clicked the dashed h, the right-angle square, and the base extension are hidden; the triangle outline and side labels remain. Highlight the constructed right triangle (α-foot-β region) with a light fill when the altitude is shown. Keep the interior of the triangles unfilled otherwise."
}

